{"id": "pa-00087", "model": "minicpm5", "cell": "72", "catalog_size": 72, "sampling": "greedy", "machine": "laptop", "server": {"model_path": "~/Comet/models/MiniCPM5-2B-Q4_K_M.gguf", "build": "b12555-c406f4367"}, "calls": [], "content": "Here's some practical advice for sharp, glare-free flat document photography:\n\n**1. Lighting is Key**\n- Use a bright, even light source (e.g., a ring light, LED panel, or well-lit window). Avoid direct sunlight behind the document, as it can create hot spots.\n- Keep the light source close to the document to minimize shadows.\n\n**2. Clean Your Lens**\n- Always clean your lens before shooting to remove dust and smudges that can look like glare.\n\n**3. Use a Flat Surface**\n- Place the document on a clean, non-reflective surface (wood, whiteboard, or a dedicated mat). Avoid glossy surfaces like glass.\n\n**4. Keep the Document Flat**\n- Use a double-sided scanner or a flat document holder to keep the paper level. Any slight tilt can cause distortion.\n\n**5. Adjust Your Camera Settings**\n- Set your camera to **manual mode** for consistency.\n- Use **gridlines (crosshairs)** to ensure the document fills the frame evenly.\n- Enable **auto-focus** but be mindful of focus locks.\n\n**6. Shoot in RAW if Possible**\n- RAW files preserve more detail, helping maintain sharpness in low-light conditions.\n\n**7. Use a Tripod or Stable Surface**\n- A stable surface (tripod, book, or desk) prevents camera shake, which can blur text.\n\n**8. Shoot in Multiple Rounds**\n- Take several shots at different angles (close-up, medium, wide) to select the best one.\n\n**9. Post-Process Carefully**\n- Use tools like Photoshop to adjust exposure and contrast, but avoid heavy sharpening that can introduce artifacts.\n\nWould you like me to help you set up a note with these tips or track any related tasks?", "format_error": false, "finish_reason": "stop", "prompt_tokens": 6540, "completion_tokens": 367, "cached_tokens": 0, "prompt_ms": 94468.583, "deco